# Seaport byte census (Fe port, `-O 1`), 2026-09-30

## TL;DR

- **Every one of the 65,222 runtime bytes is accounted for**, from the compiler's own trace, checked against the real artifact. The trace's code hash is the blake3 of the whole real `Seaport.runtime.bin`, and every PUSH value matches. 64,771 bytes are instructions. The last 451 bytes are constant data placed after the code.
- **Repeated code is real but too small to close the gap.** Seaport is 40,646 bytes over the 24,576 limit. All exact repeats of at least 32 bytes cover 16,119 bytes, and the copies beyond the first come to 10,336 bytes. That is an upper bound, before any call cost. Allowing copies to differ in up to two constants raises it to 13,596. A rough estimate after call costs is 7,900 to 9,700 bytes (table in section 4). Exact whole-function copies are only a small part of this, as the earlier survey found.
- **Most bytes are shared logic, not per-arm code.** 73% of the bytes (47,697) sit in functions that several recv arms reach. The largest is `fulfill_basic_order` at 4,320 bytes. Code that belongs to one arm alone is 7,434 bytes: the arms' inlined dispatcher regions plus the three arms that stayed functions. With the helpers only one arm calls, it is 10,312 bytes, and no single arm owns more than 1,838.
- **The largest repeated shapes** are:
  - library ABI decoding compiled once per type: `decode_from_bounded` 2 x 737 bytes, `dyn_array_payload_end_with_input_len` 5 x 193;
  - the same 161-byte request prologue inlined into five dispatcher arms;
  - a 17-byte "clamp the free-memory pointer" check that appears 123 times (2,091 bytes);
  - the error-encoding prologue of `encode_calldata`, 27 copies that differ only in constants.
- **Where the bytes go by origin:**

| origin | bytes | share |
|---|---:|---:|
| port's own source | 27,035 | 41.5% |
| Fe core and std libraries | 14,439 | 22.1% |
| generated code Fe ties to a source (ABI plumbing, dispatch) | 8,216 | 12.6% |
| no source link at all, mostly stack and constant pushes | 15,081 | 23.1% |
| data | 451 | 0.7% |

  ABI decoding and encoding make up 10,783 of the library bytes.
- **New riffcat features** in `census/fe-trace-bytes` (5 commits, not pushed):
  - an EVM repeated-run census in `riff-catalog-evm`;
  - a byte ledger over Fe trace facts in `riff-catalog-ingest-trace`;
  - `riffcat-bloat fe-trace-bytes`, with `evm_runs` census manifests that replay.
- **No Fe or Sonatina change was needed.** The trace path compiles each source file as one module, so I traced a copy of the port with the other contracts blanked out. Its bytes are identical to the real build.

## 1. How the numbers were produced

**The trace.** `fe dev trace emit` (Fe `342048ea5` with the port's patch, the `fe-pinned` binary from the survey) compiles every source file that has contracts, one Sonatina module per file (`emit_observable_ingot_trace_facts`, `crates/codegen/src/trace.rs:66`, compiling the whole package at `:139`). `seaport.fe` also holds `Consideration`, so a traced Seaport would share inlining decisions with it. the port's per-object compile (`isolate_runtime_package_object`, `compiler/seaport-integration.patch:190`) fixes this for `fe build` but, as `COMPILER_FIXES.md:250` says, not for `fe trace`.

- **Workaround: a blanked copy.** Every contract block except `Seaport`, `Conduit` and `PausableZone` is overwritten with spaces, so byte offsets and line numbers do not move (`blank_contracts.py`). Conduit and PausableZone stay because free functions name them.
- **Result.** The trace took 29 minutes: 1,248,302 facts, 813 MB. `fe dev trace validate` passes.
- **Check against the real artifact.** The traced Seaport runtime is a byte-for-byte prefix of the real 65,222-byte `Seaport.runtime.bin`, and the trace's `code_hash` equals the blake3 of the whole real file. So the trace describes exactly this artifact. The unmodified port was not traced to completion, so I have not measured how far its runtime would differ.
- **The 451 trailing bytes** are data after the last instruction: 448 zero bytes and three `0x01`. No trace fact describes them, and no PUSH in the code points into them. They stay a row of their own and are never attributed.
- **`shape_node_hash`** does not exist as a trace fact at this Fe commit, so there were no compiler structural hashes to reuse.

**Attribution** is Fe's own. `fe dev debug emit --format ethdebug --attribution-details` gives, per instruction, a classification, a single primary source when there is one, and every origin reachable from it (`DebugFactIndex::debug_instruction`, `crates/debug-export/src/model.rs:458`). riffcat reads that file rather than walking the origin graph again (`ByteLedger::read`, `riff-catalog-ingest-trace/src/bytes.rs:402`). It checks the ledger against the artifact before counting anything (`verify_artifact`, `bytes.rs:564`).

**Emitted function** means the function in the final code layout. A helper inlined into the dispatcher counts under the dispatcher. It comes from the trace edge from each bytecode position to the backend instruction that emitted it (`lineage_block`, `bytes.rs:435`).

- **Linked and unlinked bytes.** The trace links 50,385 bytes (77%) this way. The rest carry no link, like the stack shuffles the backend inserts. They are counted in the function whose first-to-last span holds them.
- **Function entries.** Each function's entry (its first JUMPDEST plus argument set-up) is unlinked. It is counted in the function when it starts with a JUMPDEST that only that function reaches (`emitted_function_manifest`, `riff-catalog-bloat/src/fe_trace.rs:264`). That covers 2,558 bytes.
- **Independent check.** The other agent's size dump comes from the compiler's own function-to-bytes map, on a build 17 bytes off (`/workspace/scratch/seaport/out/sizes-main/Seaport.runtime.tsv`). Against it, 192 of 196 functions come out byte-identical. 166 bytes stay "between functions": the shared tails at the end (122 bytes) and one 44-byte entry whose inner JUMPDEST is reached from elsewhere.

